llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
108): with MoE experts in system RAM, the GPU sits idle while the CPU
computes each layer's routed experts, and the shared expert waits for them.
Not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Token generation with the routed experts in host memory (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B or
Qwen3-Coder-Next on an 8 GB card) alternates per layer between a GPU split
(attention or DeltaNet, the router, top-k) and a CPU split (the routed
experts). The model graphs build the shared expert after the routed
experts, so the scheduler places it in the GPU split that follows the CPU
split, and before the CPU split starts the scheduler synchronizes the whole
GPU stream. The shared expert therefore runs while the CPU waits, and the
GPU idles for the whole CPU split. Its input is the FFN input, the same as
the router's, so nothing makes it wait for the routed experts. On an RX 590
the shared expert is about 80 us of GPU work per layer, 40 layers per
token; leaving it out of the graph (wrong output, timing only) took
Qwen3.6 35B-A3B generation from 23.8 to 25.2 tok/s.

The qwen35moe and qwen3next graphs now add the gated shared expert right
after the router weights (build_moe_ffn expands
moe_expand_after_weights). The scheduler computes a device split that is
followed by a host split in two parts: the head ends with the last node the
host split reads (a view's data belongs to the tensor it points into),
plus the nodes right after it that consume it, which keeps the CUDA
backend's fused top-k MoE routing in one graph. An event after the head
lets the host split copy its inputs while the tail (the shared expert)
still runs, and the CPU experts overlap it. The next device split waits
for an event recorded after the tail instead of synchronizing the stream:
the guarantee is the same, and on HIP a stream synchronization right after
a graph launch costs about 15 us more on every layer. Each graph
evaluation starts with no recorded events, so an event from an earlier
graph is never taken for one from the current graph.

Nothing is computed differently: the same kernels run on the same inputs,
in a different order on the stream. GGML_SCHED_EARLY_HOST_INPUTS=0
computes every split in one part again.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590, llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B heretic
Q4_K_M, -ncmoe 99, tg128, 5 repetitions, two rounds each, tok/s):

| HSA_ENABLE_SDMA | overlap off   | overlap on    |
| --------------- | ------------- | ------------- |
| 0               | 24.53 / 24.44 | 25.32 / 25.17 |
| 1               | 24.56 / 24.72 | 25.52 / 25.71 |

llama-perplexity (4 chunks of 512, all experts in host memory, -ub 1 and
-ub 16) with the overlap on against the GPU logits with it off: PPL
10.141204 and 10.148967 in both, mean KLD 0.000000, same top token 100%,
also with HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1. Qwen3-Coder-Next takes the same path but was
not measured: it pins 51.5 GiB of RAM. No GPU faults in any run.
